-- v9 -> v10: create the `destinationPath` index the chain never created (capability `photo-sharing`).
--
-- A REPAIR, not a new object. `Ledger.sq` has carried `CREATE INDEX ledgerRow_destinationPath` since the
-- destination column landed, but `7.sqm` added that column with `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN`, which creates
-- NO index. So a device created fresh has the index and a device that UPGRADED through 7.sqm does not —
-- two different schemas, both called current. This file makes the migration chain produce what the CREATE
-- statements produce.
--
-- NOBODY NOTICED because the check that exists to notice was inert: `verifyCommonMainLedgerDatabaseMigration`
-- verifies by applying migrations to a committed `.db` snapshot, none was committed, and a task with nothing
-- to compare reports success. Measured before the snapshot landed: a migration adding a column absent from
-- `Ledger.sq` passed it green. The snapshot is committed as of this change, so this migration is the first
-- thing the check actually verifies — which is why the snapshot is generated BEFORE this file exists.
--
-- NOT COSMETIC. The OS-driven upload tier resolves every returned `PHAssetResourceUploadJob` to its row by
-- matching the job's destination path against this column (`background-upload`), in a process the OS
-- invokes with a DEADLINE. Without the index that lookup is a full table scan per returned job — on exactly
-- the devices that have been joined longest, which are the ones with the most rows to scan.
--
-- ROW-PRESERVING, like 2.sqm through 7.sqm, and for the same load-bearing reason: a surviving COMPLETED row
-- is what stops the next cycle re-uploading an already-stored resource. `CREATE INDEX` builds a b-tree over
-- the rows already present and rewrites none of them — it settles nothing, changes no state, and creates
-- ZERO upload work. A row that was pending stays pending; a row that was COMPLETED stays COMPLETED.
--
-- `IF NOT EXISTS` IS REQUIRED, and is not defensive coding. The omission split the v9 population into two
-- genuinely different shapes, and this migration runs on BOTH:
--   * a database MIGRATED through 7.sqm has no index — this creates it;
--   * a database CREATED FRESH at v7/v8/v9 was built from `Ledger.sq`'s CREATE statements, which have
--     always carried the index — so it already has one, and a bare `CREATE INDEX` would fail with
--     "index ledgerRow_destinationPath already exists", crashing the app on update for every recent
--     install.
-- The verify task caught exactly this, on the first migration it ever verified, because the committed
-- snapshot IS a fresh-at-v9 database. SQLite normalizes `IF NOT EXISTS` out of `sqlite_master.sql`
-- (measured), so both routes still end up byte-identical to `Ledger.sq`'s statement — which is what the
-- verify task compares. Idempotent, and no drop-and-recreate is needed to get there.
--
-- WHAT THIS MIGRATION IS NOT VERIFIED BY: the snapshot is a fresh-at-v9 database, so the verify task
-- exercises the no-op half only. The half that does the work — an upgraded database gaining the index —
-- has no snapshot to represent it and is asserted by `LedgerServiceTest` instead, which seeds a
-- v9 database WITHOUT the index. Both halves are tested there; only one is checkable from a snapshot.
--
-- DOWNGRADE: the staged stance the ledger's migrations carry, and trivial here — this migration has no
-- Kotlin counterpart, so a revert of this change keeps 9.sqm and changes nothing above it. The index is
-- harmless to a build that does not know about it; SQLite maintains it regardless.
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ledgerRow_destinationPath ON ledgerRow(destinationPath);
